#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e13ab9-4bd7-5fca-bdbe-a025c1b5a20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308619} {:answer/id #uuid "e01b0af9-30cd-50d7-a4ce-8a4cab6010b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308620} {:answer/id #uuid "51aa0b61-8028-55c6-8326-35c05b009b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308621} {:answer/id #uuid "353adb76-84b2-5e81-b056-bcf86ce6ad7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308622} {:answer/id #uuid "814c8c66-e968-59ae-9762-36d350fd32ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e01b0af9-30cd-50d7-a4ce-8a4cab6010b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308620} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a2f420-7744-54b9-a957-1e22206a4137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a519148-1dbb-52b2-8341-8fad4a34af30" :question/math-academy-id "q-331913" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49dfca2-94c5-548e-9baf-9a2ef97c8f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-3{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308626} {:answer/id #uuid "29ea374a-ef1b-5dd5-b782-6931263baaee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308627} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cea147-d03b-5fc5-9000-9eae8db49959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308628} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3b017d-f699-5661-a43c-7724f21dea07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308629} {:answer/id #uuid "06043398-b0d0-5555-9f49-23528d3e351c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+2x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3b017d-f699-5661-a43c-7724f21dea07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308629} :answer-field/id #uuid "d95ee038-e4e0-565c-9eea-daade6a92927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09b19c17-c03f-56ab-a743-01f7ab8d8701" :question/math-academy-id "q-332550" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+2x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =4,\\gamma =-7.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf225a9f-68f4-5c7b-87f3-dfde75d31e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308633} {:answer/id #uuid "dd622a14-745d-5bc4-82e9-e678a61adfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308634} {:answer/id #uuid "7a20d413-26f0-53ee-bc1a-c4257a359af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf58d12-f56b-5546-befb-36d9d93221da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c0e438-2e2c-5a8e-abc3-67f8997c9f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21fc98d5-a07a-53e9-b823-65887e0c4938" :question/math-academy-id "q-332552" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fce7db3-d92c-5ce9-98d7-1a029894054e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} {:answer/id #uuid "ece7ff4a-4703-5d30-a758-2d0eaa64f87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fce7db3-d92c-5ce9-98d7-1a029894054e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dda6553-3ca9-5906-bdf1-513ea7bac620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2079c55-b54d-5179-8b5d-5fa6fd0d212f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332756" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1,\\,y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha x+(\\alpha +3\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3(0)}-2(0)+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b887604-2cc7-5490-a98e-ba00780d40f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b887604-2cc7-5490-a98e-ba00780d40f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308644} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f821175-a091-589f-be58-2ee16889c49a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab36fa8-faf8-5e3a-8a1b-c1cff952273e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332782" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=10,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=10\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\beta =10\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-5x}+2.\n$$"}]}
